;; qubes-guix-channels: extra Guix channels for a new Guix qube
;; (qubes-guix-create --unattended, --finish-install, --bootstrap).
;; qubes-guix-config writes yours (nonguix, if you want it), in
;; ~/.config/qubes-guix in dom0; or copy this there and edit it, or pass
;; another file with --channels FILE. While everything here is commented out, no extra
;; channels are added.
;;
;; Only (channel ...) forms; ';' starts a comment. They end up in the
;; qube's /etc/config.scm as #:extra-channels, so every 'guix pull' and the
;; system's own guix include them, and the packages file (qubes-guix-packages)
;; can name their packages. Give each an introduction: Guix then
;; authenticates the channel's commits as it does for its own.
;;
;; Take a channel's name, URL and introduction from its own documentation,
;; not from here, so you check them at their source. For example nonguix
;; (non-free software, e.g. firefox-esr): see the "Installation" section of
;; https://gitlab.com/nonguix/nonguix and copy its channel form below.
;;
;; (channel
;;  (name 'CHANNEL-NAME)
;;  (url "https://...")
;;  (branch "master")
;;  (introduction
;;   (make-channel-introduction
;;    "FIRST-SIGNED-COMMIT"
;;    (openpgp-fingerprint "SIGNING KEY FINGERPRINT"))))
;;
;; A channel with its own substitute server (nonguix has one) needs that
;; server and its signing key in the qube's config.scm too, by hand:
;;   #:extra-substitute-urls '("https://...")
;;   #:extra-authorized-keys (list (plain-file "x.pub" "(public-key ...)"))
;; Without them its packages are built from source.
